# spa-new coding style

Write professional code that still reads like a careful mid-level engineer wrote it: clear names, short functions, obvious flow. Avoid clever abstractions.

## Architecture

1. **Route / page** — fetch data, unwrap with `getResponseData`, pass props down.
2. **Feature `helpers.ts`** — map API shapes into simple view data.
3. **Components** — render props only. No fetch. No API mapping.

## API layer (`src/api`)

- Put shared request logic in `client.ts` (`apiGet` / `apiPost`).
- Keep each `*Service.ts` file tiny: one path per function.
- Do not copy-paste try/catch in every service file.
- Prefer plain comments like `// home page data` over long architecture essays.

## Helpers & content

- Use `asArray` / `getResponseData` from `src/lib/content.ts`.
- Keep feature helpers next to the feature (`WhoWeAre/helpers.ts`, `Projects/helpers.ts`, …).
- One helper = one job. Name functions after what they return (`mapPartners`, `getWhatWeDoItems`).

## Components

- Prefer props like `section` + `items` over digging into raw API objects in JSX.
- Early return when there is nothing to show.
- Short top-of-file comments only when the file’s role is not obvious.

## Writing style

- Readable > clever.
- Explicit > abstract.
- Small files > giant “utils” dumps.
- Do not add layers “just in case”.
- Match existing naming in the folder before inventing new patterns.
